  "worked_solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $16={4}^{2}$ and $64={4}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({4}^{2})}^{b}={({4}^{3})}^{b+4}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2b}={4}^{3(b+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(4)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2b & =3(b+4) \\\\ 2b & =3b+12 \\\\ b & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
